nel this is now a shift change  time there's firefighters out in the  line overnight watching the fire  watching for flare-ups overnight they  come in  to get some sleep during the day while  the well the daytime crews are now  getting some meals getting briefed  getting ready to head out back into the  hills here or where this fire continues  to grow now when we say the Mendocino  fire is the largest fire in California  history of sort of learning what that  means because it's not the most  dangerous fire can you sort of explain  the difference between this fire and  some of the other fires that are burning  in California well certainly this isn't  the most damaging fire in California  history by any means something like 75  residences have been destroyed and  clearly that's a disaster for those  people but there have been fires in  California in recent years that have  destroyed thousands of homes this fire  is burning in largely in remote a very  hilly rugged countryside there are  communities there are individual homes  there are certainly properties ranches  things like that in these hills but as  far as property damage goes this is this  is a rather minor fire as far as  property damage goes as far as the  amount of land that has been burned it's  clearly the largest in the in the  state's history but that doesn't mean  it's done the most property damage by  any means in your story you mentioned  what the president had to say about a  problem with water access and officials  there saying no the problem is climate  change in fact five of the largest fires  in California out of the five largest  ones that rather four of them have  burned since 2012 so you know the  experts that you've talked to do they  feel like this is the new norm or the  new norm is actually worse than what  we're seeing yes the new norm in  California is a fire season that goes  all year and fire officials say here  that the cause of that clearly is  climate change that summers are hotter  